A||N||14||87ECO5||Push-button "START!"||||5||
Z||000000||87ECO5||01-15-2001||02:37 PM||||Hope this hasn't been covered already ...<P>How hard would it be to convert a Fiero to use a start button ... a la Z8 or S2000?<P>I was thinking about the use of a decklid release switch (dressed up with proper graphics) somehow wired to the ignition system in the column.<P>Impossible?||||reg||5
Z||000001||1FST2M6||01-15-2001||02:58 PM||||the only thing you would have to deal with is the steering colum lock.. everything else is straigt forward.. the momentary switch(start button) to activate the solenoid and poof the cars running.. but even on the MGs, E-Types, S2000s and such you still need a key to put power to the push button. or just run a constant hot lead to it... but then you run the risk of somone else finding out how easy it is to start and breaking the steering colum (steering lock) and driving your car away.. (extreme case) on non-defrost equipped car you couls use a defrost switch to do this..   my $.02||||reg||1
Z||000002||Seanh||01-15-2001||04:20 PM||||If you do it the way I am thinking of, you would have to run a wire from the negative side of your ignition coil, so the starter will disenguage once the car is running. I thought of this once myself, but didnt go any further then that. It would be simple enough to do, but just havent tried it yet.||||reg||1
Z||000003||007DOUG||01-15-2001||05:03 PM||||I had that "option" in an old 84 coupe I once owned. May she rest in peace.<P>I had to bust the steering column (Don't ask. I WAS my beater though). After a while, I got tired of fishing my hand down through the column to start the car. I thought it would be neat to install a push button where the trunk release switch would be. I wired everything directly from the column. I had a flip switch that turned the igntion on, and a push button that activated the starter. When you wanted to shut the car off, just flip the switch.<P>I ran into only one problem. I used too small a push button, and melted the switch internally. Make sure you buy a heavy duty push button.||||reg||6
Z||000004||BOILERMAKER||01-15-2001||05:43 PM||||It certainly is a "do-able" job and one that shouldn't take much electrical expertise, but then again I have never tackled the task you are asking about.  I honestly don't really know why you would want to do it, but it is certainly possible.<P>Maybe you should use that switch for the oil slick, headlight machine guns, or the fog machine in the car, Q!||||reg||1
Z||000005||DrRodey||01-15-2001||05:52 PM||||007..<BR>Could you E-mail me with a wiring diagram of how you did that?||||reg||1
Z||000006||Smokin87||01-15-2001||08:13 PM||||It is defenatly possible.  and it is not that hard.  the only problem, which i think was stated, is the steering column lock.  this is not a hard job though.  just just a "dummie" key to dissable the steering column. this step is kinda tricky so if you want, email me, and i will send you a detailed email of how it is done.  then the rest is just simple wiring.<BR>have fun<BR>SnazelAndBazel@aol.com||||reg||14
Z||000007||maninblack||01-15-2001||09:30 PM||||try this site <A HREF="http://www.tdsasports.com/" TARGET=_blank>http://www.tdsasports.com/</A> ||||reg||14
Z||000008||007DOUG||01-16-2001||09:00 AM||||I don't really have a wiring. I used the schematics in the Haynes manual. I bought wire "taps" from Radio Shack and tapped into the correct wires.<P>Its really easy to do, especially when you remove the 15mm nuts/bolts and drop the column.<P>I got around the locking column because ummmmm.......well.........I had bust my column in the first place.||||reg||1
Z||000009||87ECO5||01-16-2001||09:13 AM||||Sounds like this could be a really neat/easy modification.<P>I'm not planning to bypass the locking mechanism in the column.  Heck, the S2000 still has that.  I just thought it'd be cool to have a "bright, shiny, candy-like button" (Ren and Stimpy fans will understand that) on the dash with "START!" or "GO!" or "IGNITION!" or something ...<P>Thanks for the help, folks!  PFF RULES!!!!!!!!!!!!||||reg||14
Z||000010||Monkeyman||01-16-2001||05:37 PM||||Scott--There's a company that make both lighted and non lighted push button starter switches.  If I can get the PC back without losing everything (doubtfull), I'll pull up the URL for you.  I've thought about doing this for a while but figured it would look funny to still have the steering column lock.  I wanted to do basically what Doug did.  I'd like a switch for the ignition (with the prerequisite red, flip top, aircraft style cover) and a push button starter.  If I could find a steering column that didn't have the key switch but still had tilt and would accept our TS lever, I'd do it.  I don't think it would take much money.  If you decide to do this, please email me (pics would be nice, too) and let me know how you did it.||||reg||1
Z||000011||kinboyatuwo||01-16-2001||06:07 PM||||Helped on an 87 talon a week or so ago while mine was in the shop.  It is not hard at all.  <BR>Take the advice and buy a really heavy duty switch<P>------------------<BR>"Friends don't let friends drive four doors."||||reg||5
Z||000012||MikeSchaefer||01-16-2001||11:10 PM||||Does anyone know where to buy tdasports.com's "Ignited" kits on the net?  I'm interested in seeing their prices.<BR>Thanks,<BR>Mike <A HREF="http://surf.to/fiero" TARGET=_blank>http://surf.to/fiero</A> ||||reg||5
Z||000013||Will||01-17-2001||11:20 AM||||It shouldn't be hard. Just put a block inside the steering column so that the key would not turn to the crank position. Then hijack the crank wire from teh ignition switch and run it to your start switch. You would then turn the key to the "on" position, hit your button and go.||||reg||1
Z||000014||lawrence||01-17-2001||12:40 PM||||You could use a heavy duty switch, but that will limit your options.  You'd be better off using a switch to turn on a relay or solenoid.<P>Good luck,<P>-L<BR> <A HREF="http://www.webelectricmagazine.com" TARGET=_blank>http://www.webelectricmagazine.com</A> <BR> <A HREF="http://members.nccw.net/webe/products" TARGET=_blank>http://members.nccw.net/webe/products</A>  - Products Page||||reg||1
